# open-g-hub

An open-source replacement for Logitech G Hub, for the Astro A50 X base station and headset on Windows and Linux. It talks to the base station over USB HID, with no Logitech software and no account.

Windows is tested against a real A50 X. Linux builds and is unit-tested, but has not been run against the headset yet.

## Safety

open-g-hub only sends commands that were observed from G Hub and refuses everything else. It never touches firmware and never sends a factory reset.
{% endblock %}

{% block install %}
## Install

Two programs ship together: `ogh-daemon` talks to the headset and keeps its state, and `ogh` is the command line that talks to the daemon.

### Windows

Unzip the release, put `ogh.exe` and `ogh-daemon.exe` in one folder, and run:

```sh
ogh autostart on
```

That starts `ogh-daemon.exe` at every logon. `ogh autostart off` removes it and `ogh autostart status` prints `on` or `off`. To run the daemon once without autostart, start `ogh-daemon.exe` yourself.

### NixOS

Add the flake as an input and import its module:

```nix
{
  inputs.open-g-hub.url = "github:Garulf/open-g-hub";

  outputs = { nixpkgs, open-g-hub, ... }: {
    nixosConfigurations.myhost = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
      modules = [
        open-g-hub.nixosModules.default
        { programs.open-g-hub.enable = true; }
      ];
    };
  };
}
```

This installs `ogh`, adds the udev rule that gives your user access to the base station, and runs `ogh-daemon` as a user service.

### Home Manager

```nix
{
  imports = [ open-g-hub.homeManagerModules.default ];
  services.open-g-hub.enable = true;
}
```

This installs the package and the user service. It cannot install a udev rule, so on NixOS enable the NixOS module as well, or add the package to `services.udev.packages`.

### Other Linux

Build with `cargo build --release -p ogh-cli -p ogh-daemon` and install `ogh` and `ogh-daemon` to `/usr/local/bin`. Then let your user open the base station:

```sh
sudo cp packaging/linux/70-open-g-hub.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger
```

Install the user unit and start it:

```sh
mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user
cp packaging/linux/ogh-daemon.service ~/.config/systemd/user/
systemctl --user enable --now ogh-daemon
```

The unit runs `ogh-daemon` from systemd's search path, not your shell `PATH`, so keep it in `/usr/local/bin` or edit `ExecStart=` to the full path if you installed it elsewhere.
{% endblock %}

{% block usage %}
## Usage

The daemon must be running. Every command below talks to it.

```sh
ogh status
ogh status --json
ogh watch
```

`status` shows the headset (`in_use`, `docked`, `off` or `unknown`), battery, active profile and current settings. `watch` prints a line each time something changes.

Settings:

```sh
ogh sidetone 30
ogh volume 60
ogh balance 6
ogh gate night
ogh brightness 40
ogh sleep 15
ogh mix game 70
ogh mix voice mute
ogh eq headphone preset gaming
ogh eq mic preset broadcast
ogh eq headphone gains 0 0 2 2 0 0 -1 -1 0 0
```

Profiles hold the headphone and mic EQ, noise gate, sidetone, balance and stream mix. Volume, LED brightness, Sleep After and Tones are device settings and are not part of a profile.

```sh
ogh profile save fps
ogh profile apply fps
ogh profile list
ogh profile default default
ogh profile link fps cs2.exe
ogh profile unlink cs2.exe
ogh profile delete fps
```

A linked app switches the headset to that profile while it has focus, and the default profile applies when no linked app does. Name the app by its executable file name on Windows (`cs2.exe`) and by its window class on Hyprland. Focus tracking works on Windows and on Hyprland. Elsewhere `ogh status` reports it as unavailable.

```sh
ogh autoswitch on
```

`autoswitch` moves your system default audio devices to the headset when you take it off the dock and restores them when you put it back. It is off by default.

On Hyprland, a key bind can switch profiles:

```
bind = SUPER, F9, exec, ogh profile apply fps
```

Exit codes: 2 for a bad argument, 3 when the base station is not found, 4 when G Hub is running, 5 for any other failure, 6 when the daemon is not running.
{% endblock %}

{% block extra %}
## Configuration

The daemon keeps profiles, app links and the audio switch settings in `config.toml`:

| Platform | Location |
|----------|----------|
| Windows | `%APPDATA%\open-g-hub\config\config.toml` |
| Linux | `~/.config/open-g-hub/config.toml` |

Its state and `daemon.log` sit next to it under the local data directory (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\open-g-hub\data` on Windows, `~/.local/share/open-g-hub` on Linux). Use the `ogh profile` commands to edit profiles and links. The audio switch names are the one thing you edit by hand:

```toml
[audio_switch]
enabled = false
game_output = "A50 X Game"
voice_output = "A50 X Voice"
mic_input = "A50 X Mic"
```

On Windows the game output becomes the default playback device for the Console and Multimedia roles, the voice output the default for Communications, and the mic the default capture device for all three roles. Each previous default is remembered and restored separately. On Linux the game output becomes the default sink and the mic the default source, and each name is matched as a substring of the PipeWire node description.

## Coexisting with G Hub

While G Hub's agent is running, `ogh-daemon` stays off the device. `ogh status` says `G Hub is running` (`ghub_running` in `--json`) and commands exit with code 4. Quit G Hub, both the agent and the tray icon, to use open-g-hub.
